/* ============================================================================
   poisson-approximation.epher -- the binomial for rare events as a Poisson

   Approximates the binomial probability P(X <= 3) for X with n = 1000
   trials and p = 0.002 (a rare event) by the Poisson with rate
   lambda = n * p = 2, using the builtin poissoncdf (guide 1.20). The
   rule of thumb: the Poisson approximates the binomial well when n is
   large and p small, here with n * p = 2. Both answers agree to about
   two ten-thousandths.

   Run it from a terminal:  epher "epher scripts/statistics/distributions/poisson-approximation.epher"
   In the REPL:             load epher scripts/statistics/distributions/poisson-approximation.epher
   Web/desktop:             paste the whole file into the entry, press Enter
   Everything also works line by line in the TUI.

   The knobs to edit are `n`, `p` and `k` below. Everything above the
   results section is machinery you can leave alone.
   ============================================================================ */

// ---- the knobs -----------------------------------------------------------------
const n = 1000   // trials, say components tested
const p = 0.002  // failure probability per trial
const k = 3      // the cut point: P(X <= k)

// ---- the exact binomial and the Poisson approximation ---------------------------------
print("exact binomial P(X <= 3):", binomcdf(k, n, p))
print("Poisson approximation:", poissoncdf(k, n * p))
print("difference:", binomcdf(k, n, p) - poissoncdf(k, n * p))

// ---- the parameters --------------------------------------------------------------------
print("rate lambda = n * p:", n * p)

/* ---- expected output ----
= 1000
= 0.002
= 3
= exact binomial P(X <= 3): 0.857304238915
= Poisson approximation: 0.857123460509
= difference: 0.000180778405915
= rate lambda = n * p: 2
*/